## The One Grade Rule

The most useful rule in princess cut color selection: **the princess cut reads one color grade warmer than a round brilliant of the same grade, in the same metal.**

This happens because of the chevron facets. A round brilliant spreads incoming light across 57–58 facets, diluting color across every direction.

A princess has parallel diagonal facets that channel light toward the four corners. Color concentrates there.

So if you'd buy an H-color round brilliant in platinum, you need a G in a princess. The round H and the princess G face up with equivalent corner color under normal lighting.

This single rule saves most buyers $200–$400 by avoiding the trap of going to F when G is enough.

> **Farzana's take:** When someone tells me they want a D-E color princess, I ask one question: what metal are you setting it in? If the answer is yellow gold, D color is completely wasted money. Yellow gold neutralizes one full grade of warmth at the corners. A G-VS1 in 18K yellow gold faces up colorless. You could spend $1,000 less on the stone and nobody — not even a gemologist looking at the ring from across a table — would see the difference.

---

## The 5 Color Mistakes — and the $800 They Cost

The number 5 in this guide's title is specific. Here are the 5 color-grade mistakes princess cut buyers make, and what they cost to fix.

### Mistake 1: Buying D–F Color in Yellow or Rose Gold

D, E, and F diamonds are colorless. They command a 22–62% premium over G.

In yellow or rose gold, that premium buys you nothing — the warm metal reflection neutralizes any difference between D and G at the corners. You're paying for a grade that disappears into the metal.

Cost of mistake: ~$400–$800 overspend on a 1ct stone.

### Mistake 2: Buying H Color in Platinum or White Gold

H in white metal shows corner warmth under direct sunlight and incandescent indoor lighting. It's not dramatic — but it's detectable, especially when the ring sits next to a G stone.

Some buyers are fine with it. Most are not, once they see the comparison.

Cost of mistake: reselling and upgrading the stone = $300–$600 loss plus resetting fees.

### Mistake 3: Ignoring Fluorescence as a Color Tool

Strong blue fluorescence is commonly avoided because buyers fear haziness. In reality, haziness from fluorescence occurs in a small percentage of stones and is visible in direct UV light. For princess cut stones in warm metal (yellow/rose gold), medium-strong blue fluorescence can make an I-color face up like an H.

Cost of mistake: paying H prices when a fluorescent I would have achieved the same look for $200 less.

### Mistake 4: Matching Pavé Melee to Center Stone Color Incorrectly

Pavé settings use G–H melee stones as the default. If you set an I-color princess in a G-melee pavé band, the color difference between the band stones and the center stone is visible at the junction — especially near the corners where the princess chevron path meets the first pavé stones.

Cost of mistake: visual mismatch you live with for the life of the ring.

### Mistake 5: Buying a Color Grade Without Seeing It in Context

Color grading is done face-down against a white background under controlled lighting. That setup almost never exists on someone's hand. A stone that grades H in a lab can look perfectly white in an 18K rose gold ring outdoors, and slightly warm in platinum fluorescent office lighting.

The fix: use the 360° viewer on Blue Nile and check the stone under the video's lighting conditions. If the corners look white in the video — which uses neutral indoor light — they'll look at least as good or better in warm-metal settings outdoors.

