## How to Buy Based on Color and Metal Together

The right color grade depends on which metal you're pairing. Here's a simple decision path.

**Choosing white gold or platinum?**
Buy G minimum. VS2 clarity or above.

Check the 360° viewer — if corners look warm in video, move to F. If corners look white, G is your grade.

**Choosing yellow gold (14K or 18K)?**
Buy H. You save ~$380 vs G on a 1ct stone. The warm metal reflection makes H corners face up as G-equivalent. If you find an H-VS2 under $2,000, that's your stone.

**Choosing rose gold?**
Same as yellow gold — H minimum, G if you want margin. Rose gold is slightly cooler than yellow but still provides meaningful masking.

**Considering fluorescence?**
Add medium-strong blue fluorescence as a secondary filter for I-color stones in warm metal. Fluorescent I in rose gold = H equivalent face-up. Check the 360° viewer for any haziness — most fluorescent stones show none.

> **Farzana's take:** The clients who save the most money on princess cuts are the ones who go H in rose gold and put the $380 toward better clarity or a slightly bigger stone. A [GIA 1ct H-VS2](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-search?shape=PS&a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) in an 18K rose gold solitaire is a beautiful, complete ring under $3,200. I've shown clients that ring next to a D-VS1 in platinum and asked them to guess which is which. Most can't. That's the point.

