## How to Check Corner Color Online Before Buying

Most buyers never look at the right part of the stone when using the online 360° viewer. They look at the center. The center of a princess cut disperses color well across all lighting conditions — it's the corners you need to check.

**What to do:** Open the 360° viewer on Blue Nile. Rotate the stone so you're looking at it face-up. Now zoom to one corner. Watch the corner through the full rotation.

If the corner looks white in that view, it'll look white on the hand. The 360° video uses standard indoor lighting — cooler than most office lighting and warmer than outdoor shade. It's a reliable baseline.

If the corner reads slightly yellow in the video, it will read warmer under direct sunlight and incandescent indoor lighting. Move up one color grade.

**A note on lighting conditions:** The color trap is worst in two environments: direct afternoon sunlight and warm-white indoor bulbs (2700K). If the ring will be worn primarily in office fluorescent light or outdoors in shade, the corner warmth is less pronounced. If the ring will be worn outdoors or near warm light sources often, be strict on G minimum.

**The shortcut:** Buy G in white metal and you won't need to think about this. G corners are near-colorless in all lighting conditions a ring experiences in daily wear.

---

## Lab-Grown Diamonds: Does Color Still Matter?

The Corner Color Trap applies equally to lab-grown princess diamonds. The chevron facet geometry is identical. The color grading scale is identical.

GIA and IGI grade lab princess diamonds on the same D-to-Z scale as natural stones.

What changes: the price spread between color grades.

For a natural 1ct princess, moving from G to F costs roughly $380 extra. For an equivalent lab stone, the spread between F and G is smaller — sometimes $80–$150 — because lab prices have compressed across grades as supply has increased.

This means the grade-skipping strategy is less powerful in lab diamonds. You don't save as much by dropping one grade.

The strategy still applies: G minimum in white metal. But you're making it for quality reasons, not savings.

Where lab diamonds do change the color calculation: you can afford a higher grade for the same budget. A 1.51ct D-IF lab stone at $3,006 gives you colorless at a price point where a 1ct G natural stone costs $2,536. If you're choosing lab, buy D or E — the price penalty for colorless is smaller, and you eliminate the Corner Color Trap entirely.

---

## Princess Cut Color vs. Other Diamond Shapes

The corner color minimum shifts differently across shapes. Understanding this helps when a buyer is choosing between shapes.

| Shape | Color Minimum (White Metal) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Round brilliant | H | Color disperses across 57+ facets |
| Princess cut | G | Chevron facets concentrate color at 4 corners |
| Cushion cut | G–H | Depending on facet pattern (crushed ice vs chunky) |
| Oval cut | G | Color concentrates at the tips |
| Pear cut | G | Color concentrates at the point |
| Emerald cut | G–F | Step facets show color along the table |
| Asscher cut | G | Same step facet issue as emerald |

**What this means for you:** Princess cut and round cut have a one-grade separation. If you're comparing a H-color round brilliant to a princess cut, buy G in the princess to get equivalent face-up color.

The shapes with the strictest color requirements are emerald and Asscher — their large flat step facets display body color clearly. Princess is in the middle: stricter than round, more forgiving than emerald.

