## The Color Decision Checklist

Run through this before you buy. It takes five minutes and protects you from the two most expensive color mistakes.

**1. Decide on metal first.**
Metal determines your minimum color grade. White metal = G. Yellow or rose gold = H.

Lock this in before you look at any stone.

**2. Check the stone in the 360° viewer at the corners.**
Open the stone's page on Blue Nile. Rotate to face-up. Zoom to one corner.

If the corner reads white, you're in the right color grade. If it reads warm, go up one grade.

**3. Check fluorescence if budget is tight.**
If you're close to the H/I boundary and using warm metal, filter for medium-strong blue fluorescence. A fluorescent I in rose gold faces up as H. This can save $150–$200 on a 1ct stone with no visible cost.

**4. Calculate the color premium you're paying above G.**
Take the stone's price. Find a comparable G stone at the same clarity and cut. The difference is the color premium.

If you're paying $300+ more for F over G in a yellow gold ring, that $300 buys nothing you can see. Put it toward a larger stone or better clarity.

**5. Confirm return policy before purchase.**
Blue Nile allows returns within 30 days. If the stone arrives and the corners look warmer than expected under your home lighting, you can return it and upgrade. Buy within the return window and test the stone in your intended lighting before the deadline.

